355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 193 0 R
/T (Nepal contributes a negligible share of global greenhouse gas emissions and carries a disproportionate share of the consequences. National temperature is rising by roughly 0.056 degrees Celsius per year, with the rate crossing the 0.1 threshold in the mountain region. Fifty of Nepal's seventy-seven districts are classified as high to very high vulnerability. Between 1996 and 2019, an average of 619 people died each year from climate-related impacts, and roughly 70 percent of those impacts were flood-induced. On a single day in August 2026, the temperature in Nepalgunj stood at 45 degrees Celsius while Namche recorded 12.)
/E (Nepal contributes a negligible share of global greenhouse gas emissions and carries a disproportionate share of the consequences. National temperature is rising by roughly 0.056 degrees Celsius per year, with the rate crossing the 0.1 threshold in the mountain region. Fifty of Nepal's seventy-seven districts are classified as high to very high vulnerability. Between 1996 and 2019, an average of 619 people died each year from climate-related impacts, and roughly 70 percent of those impacts were flood-induced. On a single day in August 2026, the temperature in Nepalgunj stood at 45 degrees Celsius while Namche recorded 12.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 193 0 R
/T (LCOY Nepal 2026 brought delegates from all seven provinces to Kathmandu from 14 to 16 August 2026 under the theme "Together for Climate Transparency". The conference was organized by Nepalese Youth for Climate Action \(NYCA\) and Youth Action for Sustainable and Eco Nepal \(YASEN\), with endorsement from YOUNGO. It followed nine virtual consultations that gathered climate concerns and recommendations from around 180 delegates before the residential event began.)
/E (LCOY Nepal 2026 brought delegates from all seven provinces to Kathmandu from 14 to 16 August 2026 under the theme "Together for Climate Transparency". The conference was organized by Nepalese Youth for Climate Action \(NYCA\) and Youth Action for Sustainable and Eco Nepal \(YASEN\), with endorsement from YOUNGO. It followed nine virtual consultations that gathered climate concerns and recommendations from around 180 delegates before the residential event began.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 193 0 R
/T (The programme was built around seven thematic pillars: youth and children's engagement in climate processes; loss and damage, climate finance and just transition; changing mountains and livelihoods; climate resilient education systems and Action for Climate Empowerment; agriculture, food security and nutrition; WASH and water security; and children, youth and GEDSI. Across three days, delegates attended plenary sessions, keynotes, dedicated thematic sessions, a mock COP negotiation, a people's hearing on climate justice, a climate finance pitching workshop, a province-wise grassroots case study exercise, and structured group work on Nepal's National Climate Change Policy 2019 and collectively drafted the National Children and Youth Statement on Climate Change.)
/E (The programme was built around seven thematic pillars: youth and children's engagement in climate processes; loss and damage, climate finance and just transition; changing mountains and livelihoods; climate resilient education systems and Action for Climate Empowerment; agriculture, food security and nutrition; WASH and water security; and children, youth and GEDSI. Across three days, delegates attended plenary sessions, keynotes, dedicated thematic sessions, a mock COP negotiation, a people's hearing on climate justice, a climate finance pitching workshop, a province-wise grassroots case study exercise, and structured group work on Nepal's National Climate Change Policy 2019 and collectively drafted the National Children and Youth Statement on Climate Change.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 193 0 R
/T (The conference was opened by the Honourable Minister for Agriculture, Forests and Environment, Geeta Chaudhary, who committed to carrying youth voices into every stage of policy and to COP 31. Speakers included representatives of MoAFE, MoES, the Department of Water Supply and Sewerage Management, UNICEF Nepal, WWF Nepal, ICIMOD, Clean Energy Nepal, Prakriti Resource Centre, Plan International Nepal, Forest Action, IWMI, YUWA, WOREC, BYAN, World Food Forum Nepal, Welthungerhilfe and Practical Action Nepal.)
/E (The conference was opened by the Honourable Minister for Agriculture, Forests and Environment, Geeta Chaudhary, who committed to carrying youth voices into every stage of policy and to COP 31. Speakers included representatives of MoAFE, MoES, the Department of Water Supply and Sewerage Management, UNICEF Nepal, WWF Nepal, ICIMOD, Clean Energy Nepal, Prakriti Resource Centre, Plan International Nepal, Forest Action, IWMI, YUWA, WOREC, BYAN, World Food Forum Nepal, Welthungerhilfe and Practical Action Nepal.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
