985 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (The Mock COP simulation was designed to give delegates a practical understanding of climate negotiations, consensus-building, and strategic trade-offs using a hypothetical draft decision. The exercise deliberately departed from traditional Model United Nations formats by enforcing the strict COP consensus model, where a single objecting party can block a text, compelling delegates to actively negotiate rather than out-vote opponents. Nine distinct negotiating blocs, including the Mountain Group, G77 and China, AILAC, the LDC Group, the Arab Group, the African Group, the European Union, Australia, and the LMDCs, participated in the exercise armed with bracketed texts and private instructions.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
986 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (As organizers, we structured the simulation around the core mechanics of international climate diplomacy, forcing participants to navigate complex text-based bargaining rather than writing decisions from scratch. Delegates were tasked with closing brackets by managing opening positions, fallbacks, and red lines distributed across their bloc briefs. This design compelled them to engage in rigorous cross-bloc alliances, master the art of strategic concessions, and understand how procedural rules and financial contingencies can shape the ultimate strength and outcome of a global climate decision.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
