/P 215 0 R
/E (Parallel Workshop 1: Pitching for Climate Finance, Seeking Possible Solutions for Local Climate Challenges Session )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 215 0 R
/E (Facilitated by: Hemanshu Kafle and the Organizing team)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1006 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 215 0 R
/E (The workshop challenged eight groups to translate grassroots environmental problems into fundable, 12 months project proposals within a strict 60 minute timeframe. Working from standardized project canvases, pitch cards, peer scorecards, and a reference sheet of 32 youth and grassroots climate funds, participants had to distill their initiatives into concise 90-second pitches evaluated by their peers on five core criteria: urgency, specificity, stated change, community roots, and clarity.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1007 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 215 0 R
/E (The facilitator guided the room through the mechanics of effective donor pitching, highlighting why proposals typically fail, such as describing activities without naming concrete changes, asking for unspecified funding amounts, or wasting precious time on background information. Four completed project canvases were collected, showcasing distinct local challenges, innovative problem-solving approaches, and the common hurdles young climate innovators face when approaching donors:)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1008 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2634 0 R
/E (Pokhari Pulse focused on reviving traditional ponds and stone spouts in the Kathmandu Valley to mitigate flood and drought risks. While it stood out as the most technically rigorous proposal integrating engineering designs with indigenous Guthi institutional knowledge and an open source replication manual its execution was undermined by an inconsistent financial ask, displaying conflicting figures and currencies across its canvas and pitch card.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2635 0 R
/E (Waste to Worth targeted poor waste management and segregation along the Hanumante Khola corridor in Kageshwori Manohara Municipality through community trainings and terrace farming initiatives. It delivered the strongest and most measurable impact statement of the session \(reducing visible waste dumping\), fitting neatly within grant windows like Roots Microgrants or the Spark Fund, though it omitted naming a target fund on its canvas.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1010 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2636 0 R
/E (Jaldiwan tackled invasive water hyacinth choking the Jagdishpur Tal wetland by converting harvested biomass into extracted fiber for ropes, mats, and baskets. Representing the cleanest fund match of the workshop, it aligned perfectly with the Youth4Climate: Call for Solutions ceiling of USD 30,000 and offered the most bulletproof defense against critical funder inquiries by positioning the weed as a flexible raw material.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1011 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2637 0 R
/E (Climate Resilient and Sustainable Vegetable Enterprise, Bharatpur designed a sophisticated blended finance model for 200 smallholder farmers facing erratic rainfall, splitting its large NRs 5,000,000 ask evenly across a donor fund, local government, and the farmers themselves. Despite its strong baseline context and community backing, the proposal faltered because it left its 12 month change unstated and requested an amount far exceeding the ceiling of the target fund it selected.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
