1032 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2651 0 R
/E (Government stakeholders defended the project by asserting that a formal EIA was complete, the designated site involved minimal impact, and post-construction tree planting would benefit locals. They emphasized that the project cleared all three tiers of government, promised ongoing compensation, and would reduce congestion at Tribhuvan International Airport while creating local jobs.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1033 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2652 0 R
/E (Affected communities pushed back by highlighting their total reliance on the forest and questioning why their land was targeted over alternatives. They challenged authorities on cultural preservation, criticized the government for favoring private interests, and countered with a proposal to develop tourism routes instead.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1034 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2653 0 R
/E (CSOs and activists questioned the validity of the EIA and the justification for displacing indigenous populations without prior consent. They highlighted critical timing gaps, noting that newly planted trees take decades to replace mature forests, while challenging habitat loss and citizen rights.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1035 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2654 0 R
/E (Youth groups argued that artificial tree planting cannot restore lost biodiversity and that the state should instead prioritize upgrading existing public infrastructure like schools and hospitals. They also introduced a demographic critique, arguing that a massive international airport facilitating outward migration accelerates local population loss.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1036 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (The session concluded with a reflective discussion on the dual role humans play as both protectors and destroyers of the environment, reinforcing that constructive coordination between local populations and government authorities is completely unavoidable. Delegates walked away recognizing that high-stakes public hearings demand rigorous, evidence-based answers to tough questions, ultimately demonstrating how diverse sectors negotiate toward problem-solving and where meaningful youth engagement fits within national decision-making.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
