>>
endobj
1111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2703 0 R
/E (Climate Finance: Proper utilisation of local resources; compulsory inclusion of Corporate Social Responsibility \(CSR\); and ensuring climate finance reaches the ground level.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2704 0 R
/E (Changing Mountains and Livelihoods: Innovative integration of disaster risk reduction \(DRR\) and mechanisms to channel migration remittances towards adaptation funding.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2705 0 R
/E (Agriculture: Post-harvest mapping; integration of remote sensing and AI; development of food zones; promotion of traditional knowledge; and adoption of circular economy approaches.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2706 0 R
/E (Gender Equality, Disability and Social Inclusion \(GEDSI\): Accessible, inclusive, and commonly adaptable spaces; inclusion of diverse disability groups in policymaking; and establishment of an equitable platform for meaningful participation.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (Overall, the session emphasised that youth-centred climate governance must move from recognition to meaningful participation and from policy commitments to measurable local action, supported by disaggregated data, dedicated resources, inclusive institutions, capacity building, and accountability.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
